      "text": " <think>\nTo solve the problem of finding the product of 164 and 165, we can use the distributive property of multiplication over addition. This property allows us to break down the multiplication into simpler parts. Specifically, we can express 165 as 164 + 1. Therefore, the product of 164 and 165 can be written as:\n\\[ 164 \\times 165 = 164 \\times (164 + 1) \\]\nUsing the distributive property, we can expand this expression:\n\\[ 164 \\times (164 + 1) = 164 \\times 164 + 164 \\times 1 \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ 164 \\times 165 = 164^2 + 164 \\]\nNext, we need to calculate \\(164^2\\). We can find this by using the formula for the square of a binomial:\n\\[ 164^2 = (160 + 4)^2 \\]\nExpanding this using the binomial theorem:\n\\[ (160 + 4)^2 = 160^2 + 2 \\times 160 \\times 4 + 4^2 \\]\nCalculating each term separately:\n\\[ 160^2 = 25600 \\]\n\\[ 2 \\times 160 \\times 4 = 1280 \\]\n\\[ 4^2 = 16 \\]\nAdding these together:\n\\[ 160^2 + 2 \\times 160 \\times 4 + 4^2 = 25600 + 1280 + 16 = 26896 \\]\nSo, \\(164^2 = 26896\\). Now we substitute this back into our expression:\n\\[ 164 \\times 165 = 164^2 + 164 = 26896 + 164 \\]\nFinally, we add these two numbers:\n\\[ 26896 + 164 = 27060 \\]\nTherefore, the product of 164 and 165 is:\n\\[ 164 \\times 165 = 27060 \\]\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe product of 164 and 165 is 27060.\n</answer>",
